        "thinking": "I'm identifying the root as kṣiv (bhvādi, parasmaipada, meaning \"to throw/discard\"), noting some ambiguity in its exact form, then working out the intensive (yaṅ) formation with reduplication.\n\nWorking through the kuhoścuḥ substitution, guṇa of the reduplicated syllable, and the hali ca lengthening rule, I arrive at cekṣīvya as the yaṅ stem. Then applying ṇic, the yasya halaḥ rule deletes the ya after a consonant, leaving cekṣīv + i, and I'm now checking whether guṇa or vṛddhi applies to the long ī upadhā in this causative formation.\n\nI'm trying a similar case with paṭh: after ṇic and ato lopa, the 'ya' deletion removes the whole syllable, so I need to determine whether that lopa counts as sthānivat for purposes of the earlier operation — essentially whether the vṛddhi/guṇa of the upadhā gets blocked once 'ya' is gone.\n\nRight, I'm recalling that sūtra 1.1.4, na dhātulopa ārdhadhātuke, blocks guṇa or vṛddhi precisely when that guṇa/vṛddhi would be caused by an ārdhadhātuka affix that itself triggers dhātu-lopa — which explains why bebhiditā retains the short form instead of undergoing guṇa.\n\nFor cekṣiv, since ṇic causes loss of ya, there's no guṇa triggered there, but the śap-induced guṇa of the ṇic's i still applies, giving cekṣivay-, and then 7.3.101 (ato dīrgho yañi) lengthens the preceding a, yielding cekṣivayāvaḥ. I'm checking whether hali ca (8.2.77) could apply here, but since v is followed by a vowel rather than a consonant, that rule doesn't trigger, so the form stays as cekṣivayāvaḥ.\n\nNow prefixing ni-, there's no ṇatva trigger since there's no retroflex-conditioning consonant after it, so it combines straightforwardly: निचेक्षिवयावः. I'm also verifying the abhyāsa derivation for kṣiv — from kṣi, applying halādiḥ śeṣaḥ to get ki, then kuhoścuḥ converts it to ci, and guṇo yaṅlukoḥ turns it into ce, confirming cekṣiv is correct. So the final answer is निचेक्षिवयावः.\n\n",
